WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:08.960
You probably know that plants get their energy
from the sun through their leaves in a process

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.210
called photosynthesis.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:16.200
We humans don’t have our own leaves, but
maybe we can get energy from the sun in a

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.580
different way--electrical energy, that is!

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.430
Could there be some kind of electrical “leaf”?

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:28.080
A solar cell gathers light from the sun and
generates electricity--just as a leaf generates

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.930
food for a plant--let’s find out how!

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.480
The sun emits energy in the form of waves.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.750
These waves can range in length, from short,
ultraviolet waves, through the rainbow of

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.610
the visible spectrum, to long infrared waves.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:50.410
When the sun is shining, these waves move
towards the earth and hit the surface of solar

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:51.410
cells.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.090
Let’s take a closer look!

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:59.460
The active part of a solar cell is a wafer
made of a semiconductive material, typically

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.360
silicon.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.700
A semiconductor is a type of material that
normally doesn’t conduct electricity well,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.720
but it can be made more conductive under certain
conditions.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.180
The semiconductor part of the solar cell has
three layers.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:23.010
The thin top layer contains silicon and a
very tiny amount of an element, such as phosphorus,

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.010
that has more electrons than silicon.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:31.869
This gives the top layer an excess of electrons
that are free to move and make the material

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:33.549
more conductive.

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:40.360
The top layer is also called negative-type,
or n-type, as it favors the collection and

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.180
transport of electrons.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:49.360
The thin bottom layer contains both silicon
and an element, such as boron, that has fewer

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.650
electrons than silicon.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:57.090
This gives the bottom layer fewer electrons
that are free to move, therefore making the

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.520
material less conductive for electrons.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.909
A missing electron can be described as an
effective positive charge.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:12.560
Therefore, the bottom layer is called positive-type,
or p-type, as it preferentially favors the

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:19.189
collection and transport of these positive
charges, also dubbed ‘holes.’

00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:26.079
The thicker middle layer has only slightly
fewer electrons, making it marginally p-type.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:32.329
Thin metal lines, typically made of silver,
are printed on the top n-type layer, and the

00:02:32.329 --> 00:02:37.060
bottom p-type layer is in contact with an
aluminum plate.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:42.799
When light waves hit the top surface of the
silicon solar cell, only light with wavelengths

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:50.840
from a specific window of the solar spectrum
(350-1140 nm) are absorbed into the middle

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.829
layer of the solar cell.

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:59.199
This range of wavelengths includes the visible
spectrum: ultraviolet wavelengths are so short

00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:05.319
they stop at the surface, and infrared wavelengths
are so long they cannot be absorbed and pass

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:09.389
right through the cell or are reflected back.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:16.269
The light wave knocks an electron off a silicon
atom, setting the electron loose and leaving

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:22.239
an area of positive charge (a ‘hole’)
where the electron used to be.

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:27.680
The loose electron then moves towards the
top and reaches the top n-type layer, which

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.939
readily accepts electrons.

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:35.900
Similarly, the loose hole moves towards the
bottom and reaches the bottom p-type layer,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.079
which readily accepts holes.

00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:42.229
This continues as long as sunlight shines
on the solar cell.

00:03:42.229 --> 00:03:47.629
Now that the electrons and the holes have
been separated, connecting a wire between

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:53.680
the top and the bottom metal electrodes provides
a pathway for the electrons to move towards

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:54.729
the holes.

00:03:54.729 --> 00:03:58.469
Flow of electrons is electrical current!

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:02.339
One solar cell produces several Watts of power.

00:04:02.339 --> 00:04:08.019
This may be sufficient for running a calculator
or a phone charger, but it is not sufficient

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:12.090
for running a toaster, for example, which
uses one thousand Watts.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:19.410
So, several solar cells, typically 32, are
wired together to make a solar panel.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:27.930
Several solar panels are needed to generate
enough electricity to power

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:28.930
a household.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:32.439
Like leaves, solar cells are a viable way
to convert the sun’s rays directly into

00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:34.990
electricity that we can use.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:40.020
Future challenges include improving the efficiency
by which a solar cell converts sunlight to

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.180
electricity and making the solar energy cheaper.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:50.060
Of course, solar cells produce electricity
only during the day, so, storing the electricity

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:54.470
efficiently for use during nighttime is another
important challenge.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:05:00.210
Advancements in solar technologies present
the potential to power our lives, if we just

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:00.460
leave it to sun!

